# Danger zone

**Critical rule.** When editing related files, if you notice existing code or docs that violate this rule — even
outside your current task — **flag it to the user as a high concern** before or alongside your main work.

Read [DESIGN.md — Danger zone](../../DESIGN.md#danger-zone).

## Rules

- **A tool runs only from its own wizard.** Never from Write, Restore or Backup.
- **Backup first, checked.** Refuse to touch the radio until the user has ticked "I've saved both files".
- **One guarded send path per tool, with its own allow-list, as narrow as the change.** Hard interlocks are code: no
  setting, flag, env value or query parameter may disable one. Each interlock has a refusal test.
- **The range-change sender is the only code that writes LocalInfo.** The generic write helpers' hard deny of
  `0x04F00000..0x04FFFFFF` lands with #1410 (until then no caller reaches LocalInfo). Only files under
  `rangeChange/` import the sender or its extent, and only the allow-listed files build a LocalInfo frame
  (`rangeChangeBoundary.test.ts`). The sender validates its own payload; it does not trust its caller. Never address `0x04FC0000`, never send PC-link `H`, never touch sector markers.
- **Interlocks never read the decisions file** (`src/app/services/dangerZone/decisions.ts`). Decisions are copy,
  confirm method and timings; interlocks are not decisions.
- **Say "Nothing has changed" at every exit before the first write**, and offer a resumable recovery after it. Send
  `END` only when `atD890RangeChangeMayEndAfter(err)` says so; it fails closed for any error it does not know.
- **No feature flag.** There is no prod-disabled switch; the owner tests on pre-prod builds before a release.
- **The Danger zone is not Write.** Don't reuse `radioWriteEnvGate`, the Write stepper or the restore plumbing.
